Rites Held For Hogan, Ex-Catcher United Press International
BOSTON, April 10. — Funeral services will be held today for James Francis (Shanty) Hogan, who caught 13 years in the major leagues with Washington and the old New York Giants and Boston Braves. He died Friday after a long illness. 
Hogan, who turned 61 two weeks ago, was known as “‘the Babe Ruth of catchers" during his colorful catching career, He was bigger than Ruth — at 6-foot-1, 250 pounds — and was a hard hitter.
He broke in with the old Braves after graduation from Somerville High School in 1925.
He also played part of that season under Casey Stengel at Worcester in the Eastern League.
HOGAN WAS traded to the New York Giants in 1928 in a controversial deal for hitting star Rogers Hornsby. But he si-
lenced many critics by batting over .300 in four of his five seasons with John McGraw’s teams.
He was peddled back to the Braves in 1933 and finished his career with Washington in 1937. He dropped out of baseball after
retiring, returning to his hometown of Somerville.
Hogan leaves his wife.
Hogan was a teammate of Andy Cohen when they played with the New York Giants.
They were teammates in a vaudeville act during one off-season tour. Hogan visited in El Paso with Cohen one winter.

